17 in integration tests:\n```typescript\nit('17. should track event counts through the bus', async () => {\n    catalog.createProduct('Widget', 10, 'gadgets');\n    catalog.createProduct('Gizmo', 20, 'gadgets');\n\n    // Wait for async propagation\n    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10));\n\n    expect(analytics.getEventCount(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED)).toBe(2);\n});\n```\n\nThey waited 10ms, so this should be fine.\n\nBut the unit test for analytics:\n```typescript\nit('should count events by type', async () => {\n    await bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, { productId: 'p1', ... });\n    await bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, { productId: 'p2', ... });\n    expect(analytics.getEventCount(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED)).toBe(2);\n});\n```\n\nHere they directly `await bus.emit(...)` which properly waits for all handlers, so this is fine.\n\n5. **Now let me think about potential issues with the notifications service integration test**:\n\nTest 15: \n```typescript\nit('15. should send notification on order status change', async () => {\n    notifications.subscribe('user1');\n    const orderId = orders.createOrder('user1', 'cart1', 100);\n    orders.updateStatus(orderId, OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);\n    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 10);\n    // ...\n});\n```\n\nHere, `orders.createOrder(...)` calls `bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, ...)` without await, so the notification service's handler for `ORDER_CREATED` (which sets up the orderUserMap) runs asynchronously. Then `orders.updateStatus(...)` also emits without await. Both of these fire in the background.\n\nThe notification handler for `ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED` will fire asynchronously after `updateStatus`. But the `ORDER_CREATED` handler also fires asynchronously. There's a race condition: the `ORDER_CREATED` handler needs to set up `orderUserMap` BEFORE the `ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED` handler runs.\n\nIf both events fire and complete nearly simultaneously, we can't guarantee the order. But JavaScript 